3963510110499812330,DcBN3IoArPq,https://www.instagram.com/reel/DcBN3IoArPq/,reel,2026-08-14T11:36:01.000Z,212579,8816,18120120700896808,"The Constitution of India gives broad protection to different faiths, religions, traditions, customs and cultural practices, while also placing them within the framework of equality, individual rights, public order, morality and health. The key provisions are: 1. Freedom of religion — Article 25 Article 25 guarantees every person: “freedom of conscience and the right freely to profess, practise and propagate religion.” This means a person generally has the constitutional right to: Follow any faith or no faith. Practice religious beliefs. Profess or express one's religion. Propagate one's religious beliefs. However, this freedom is not absolute. It is subject to public order, morality, health, and the other fundamental rights in Part III. 2. Religious denominations — Article 26 Article 26 gives religious denominations the right, subject to public order, morality and health, to: Establish and maintain institutions for religious and charitable purposes. Manage their own affairs in matters of religion. Own and acquire property. Administer such property according to law. 3. No discrimination based on religion — Article 15 Article 15 prohibits the State from discriminating against citizens on grounds including religion, race, caste, sex or place of birth. So, the State cannot ordinarily deny a citizen equal access to public facilities merely because of their religion. 4. Equality before law — Article 14 Article 14 says: “The State shall not deny to any person equality before the law or the equal protection of the laws…” Therefore, different religious communities are constitutionally entitled to equal protection of the law. 5. Protection of religious and cultural identity — Articles 29 & 30 Article 29 protects the right of a section of citizens having a distinct language, script or culture to conserve it. Article 30 gives religious and linguistic minorities the right to establish and administer educational institutions of their choice, subject to the constitutional framework. 6. India has no State religion The Constitution does not establish Hinduism, Islam, Christianity, Sikhism, Buddhism, Jainism or any other religion as India's official State religion.",2026-08-14T17:07:52.000Z,0,0,,0,____iftikhar____,Iftikhar Ahmad,3612470241,false,https://www.instagram.com/____iftikhar____/
